messages:
violet: Strange one — browser quickstart + service-worker intercepts are causing SSE streaming to deliver events out of order for clients using fetch ReadableStream. Repro in sample-app when the SW modifies the response body (adds a comment) on the fly.

cheng: Can you paste a minimal capture? Which runtimes/browsers?

violet: Repro on Chrome stable. Sample pipeline: service worker does a small transform, inserting a `:sw-note\n\n` comment between chunks. Server emits:

```
data: {\"t\":\"A\"}\n\ndata: {\"t\":\"B\"}\n\ndata: [DONE]\n\n```

SW intercept transforms to:

- read chunk A as bytes, pass through
- insert `:sw-note\n\n` (kept as comment)
- read chunk B but sometimes the browser delivers the transformed chunks to the ReadableStream underlying the fetch body such that B arrives before the assembled A+B event boundaries are processed. Observed final tokens: [\"A\",\"\",\"B\"] or sometimes [\"B\",\"A\"].

aaron: Hmm, sounds like the stream transform semantics between the ServiceWorker and Fetch body are not preserving original chunk/event atomicity. Are you using text decoder per chunk in the client?

violet: The quickstart's browser helper uses a simple async iterator over the response.body.getReader() and calls decoder.decode(chunk, {stream:true}) per read then splits on "\n\n". With the SW inserting comments mid-multibyte frames or adding its own chunk boundary, the adapter sometimes sees the comment break the event assembly.

cheng: Two axes: 1) SW transform splitting multibyte sequences/comments, 2) client decoder+split-on-separator approach not robust to arbitrary chunk boundaries. We fixed similar issues server-side by treating EOF as a delimiter and using incremental decoders. For the browser path, we should avoid per-chunk decode+split; instead accumulate bytes and decode only when we've confirmed a full line (or use TextDecoderStream).

aaron: Another angle: the SW should preserve byte stream integrity; but in practice some transform streams re-chunk. We should harden client helpers: a byte buffer + TextDecoder with streaming=true and only split events once we've seen the double newline sequence in decoded output. Also treat lone empty data events (data: "") as no-ops so comments don't produce empty tokens.

violet: I prototyped an update to the browser helper:

1) Use a Uint8Array buffer to append chunks.
2) Use new TextDecoder('utf-8', {fatal:false}) with decode(remaining, {stream:true}) but only split on `\n\n` in the decoded text when it's observed.
3) When swapping streams (e.g., due to auth refresh), call reader.cancel(), drain remaining buffered bytes into event assembly, treat cancellation as EOF delimiter, then reconnect.

Patch diff (snip):

```js
// pseudo
let buf = new Uint8Array(0);
const td = new TextDecoder();
while (true) {
  const {value, done} = await reader.read();
  if (value) buf = concat(buf, value);
  const text = td.decode(buf, {stream: true});
  const parts = text.split('\n\n');
  // keep last partial part in buf (encode again) ...
  // emit complete parts
  if (done) {
    // treat EOF as boundary
    emit(parts.slice(0, -1));
    break;
  }
}
```

cheng: Nice. Also add a small guard: if a comment line is exactly `:sw-note` then ignore it. But don't special-case; instead drop empty data events and coalesce consecutive data events into one if they arrive without interposing `[DONE]`. That reduces surface noise for sample apps.

aaron: Add e2e tests: run the sample-app with a service worker that randomly injects comment lines and random chunk splits and assert that the final token sequence equals server output (order preserved) and no replacement or empty tokens leak. Also test fetch polyfills (e.g., older Safari shims) if possible.

qa-bot: Issue #7901 created by violet - title: browser: handle SW transform/re-chunking in fetch streaming helper; add robust byte-buffered parser + e2e test with SW-inserts

violet: PR #7890 opened with the browser helper change + e2e harness using Playwright that registers a SW to insert comments and randomly split chunks. Also added a short Quickstart note: "If using SW transforms, prefer SDK helpers with byte-aware parsing or enable sse-debug to inspect raw bytes."

cheng: Please also document that service workers may re-chunk and that SDKs assume stream is chunk-reordered tolerant. Add a small troubleshooting checklist in devex quickstart.

aaron: LGTM after tests. One nit: ensure decoder buffer bounded at, say, 256KB to avoid OOM when downstream never sends newline. Emit a debug warning if buffer grows beyond threshold.

qa-bot: PR #7890 merged to main by cheng - changelog: web-sdk: make fetch streaming helper byte-buffered and re-chunk tolerant; add Playwright e2e that simulates SW comment injection and random chunk boundaries; docs updated

violet: Verified against the sample-app in staging with SW test harness; 1000 randomized trials passed. Thanks for the quick turnaround :clap:
